TEXT -96 -88 Left 4 ;Sawtooth generator
TEXT -88 -40 Left 2 ;Ramp-up sawtooth (i.e. rising)\nInput is a pulse (the edge sig), and Iref
TEXT 616 368 Left 0 ;PMOS current mirror
TEXT 544 512 Left 0 ;Timing capacitor
TEXT 320 544 Left 0 ;NMOS discharge switch
TEXT 192 320 Left 0 ;PMOS diode-connected
TEXT 568 -48 Left 2 !.inc library.mod
TEXT 152 616 Left 0 ;NMOS: larger width for faster discharge (lower ON-resistance),\nsince we have a very short pulse
TEXT 160 272 Left 0 ;PMOS: 10x larger width to get 0.1xIref\n(to then make the salve PMOS charge the cap slower)
TEXT 760 336 Left 0 ;PMOS: to avoid Channel Length Modulation, increase lengths\n(both the same amount to keep the 10:1 ratio)\ntransistor longer to increase its output resistance\nset to 2u to increase r_o ~6x
TEXT 704 536 Left 1 ;time to reach comp threshold: t (set in project cosntraints)\nthreshold voltage for comp: V_th (to compute)\ncurrent mirror ratio: k = 0.1 (10:1)\n \nt = ( C * V_th ) / ( k * Iref )     => pulse width is directly inversely proportional to Iref!\n \nif we take t=4.5us for Iref=1uA, we can use a V_th=1.65V as it's\nthe switching point of an inverter, we get then a C/k ratio of 2.727pF\nif we keep our k=0.1 we can then know we need to use a 272.7fF cap value\nBUT it's slower than supposed to (0.68us rather than 0.5us for 9uA => 0.5/065=0.735),\nso lets use 272.7fF * 0.735 = 200.5fF => adjusted value for the cap
TEXT 360 200 Center 0 ;10/1 ratio\n<------------>
